      "category": "body",
      "title": "Cab corners, floor pans and rocker panels rust out at the cab mounts",
      "description": "The 67-72 C10 cab is a rust trap. The box-section cab supports run from the body mounts to the inner rockers and are spot-welded to both the inner rocker and the floor, so when the rockers and cab corners pack with debris and moisture (the rockers are literally cup-shaped pockets along the bottom of the cab) they rot from the inside out and take the structural cab supports with them. Cab corners behind the rockers are 'almost always rotten,' the lower door pillars rust where the hinges bolt on, and once the supports corrode the cab loses rigidity and can twist/sag on its mounts. This is the single most common reason a 'cheap' C10 turns into a major project.",
      "solution": "Don't chase pinholes. Restorers cut out and weld in full reproduction cab corners, rocker panels (outer AND inner rocker supports), and a complete one-piece floor pan rather than patching, then re-weld the box-section cab supports so the cab is structurally sound again. Critically: brace the door openings with 1x1 steel tubing before cutting so the cab can't twist while panels are out, restore/replace the rubber cab mount bushings, and after metalwork seal every seam, spray cavity wax/internal frame coating inside the rockers and supports, and re-open the factory drain holes so the new metal doesn't rot from the inside again.",
      "severity": "high",
      "confidence": "high",
      "symptoms": [
        "Rust bubbling and holes at the lower cab corners and rocker panels",
        "Soft/perforated floor pans under the carpet and pedals",
        "Doors sagging or not latching as cab support sags",
        "Rust at the bottom of the door hinge pillars",
        "Water/dust intrusion into the cab"
      ],
