      "category": "body",
      "title": "Drop-center frame rust-through at the body mounts",
      "description": "The drop-center frame that gives the 67-72 its low stance also traps moisture and mud in all the wrong places — most notably where the body mounts bolt through the frame rail and around the rear kick-up over the axle. Decades of trapped grime rust the rails from the inside; by the time it shows on the outside the mount areas can be paper-thin, which is dangerous because the cab/bed mounts and trailing-arm pickups all load those sections. A rotten frame is the difference between a restorable truck and a parts truck.",
      "solution": "Sandblast or wire-wheel the frame bare and inspect the body-mount and rear kick-up areas with a pick. Repair perforated sections with boxing/repair plate (or source a solid frame from a dry-climate truck), replace ALL the rubber body-mount bushings while it's apart, then protect it for good: internal frame coating sprayed inside the rails through the access holes plus an epoxy-primer/chassis-coat outside. Restorers doing a frame-off take the chance to media-blast, repair, and seal the whole frame so it never rots again.",
      "severity": "high",
      "confidence": "medium",
      "symptoms": [
        "Flaky/scaly rust and perforation at the body-mount pads",
        "Frame sounds dull/thin when tapped near the mounts",
        "Crushed or rotten body mount bushings",
        "Visible rot at the rear frame kick-up over the axle"
      ],
