      "category": "electrical",
      "title": "External-regulator generator/alternator charging and points ignition are unreliable",
      "description": "Early 67 trucks used a generator and the rest used a low-output, externally-regulated alternator with a separate mechanical voltage regulator that ages out and either over- or under-charges. Combined with 50-year-old brittle wiring, dirty bulkhead connectors, and breaker-point/condenser ignition that drifts out of tune (dwell and timing wander, points pit and burn), the result is hard starting, dim lights, dead batteries and inconsistent running. Modern accessories (electric fans, stereo, EFI swaps) overwhelm the original output entirely.",
      "solution": "Convert to a modern internally-regulated single-wire/one-wire alternator (a 10SI/CS-130 style 100A+ unit) using a conversion harness — this deletes the failure-prone external regulator and supports modern loads. On the ignition side, the standard bulletproofing move is an HEI distributor conversion (or a points-to-electronic module) which gives a hotter, maintenance-free spark and stable timing; run the proper 12-gauge switched feed to the HEI. While in there, restorers refresh the bulkhead connector and brittle engine-bay harness so charging and ignition are dead reliable.",
      "severity": "medium",
      "confidence": "medium",
      "symptoms": [
        "Battery goes dead / won't hold charge",
        "Ammeter swinging or dim, flickering lights",
        "Hard starting and rough idle as points wear",
        "Need to re-gap points and re-time frequently",
        "Engine 'run-on' or no-charge after HEI/alternator swaps wired wrong"
      ],
