      "category": "brakes",
      "title": "Four-wheel drum brakes (1967-1970) fade and pull",
      "description": "1967-1970 C10s used four-wheel drum brakes on a single-circuit-feel system that is marginal by modern standards. The drums fade badly with repeated or downhill stops, pull to one side when the shoes/wheel cylinders wear unevenly, and the long pedal feels vague — a real safety liability on a heavy truck driven in modern traffic. (Chevrolet only switched the 2WD trucks to front discs and the 5-lug pattern for 1971.)",
      "solution": "The proven restorer upgrade is a front drum-to-disc conversion kit that bolts to the stock 6-lug spindles, paired with a correct dual-bore disc/drum master cylinder and proportioning valve (and a power booster if desired). This gives fade-free, straight, modern stopping. Many builders go a step further to a 5-lug disc spindle set to open up modern wheel choices. At minimum, restore the drums with new shoes, wheel cylinders, springs and a fresh dual master, but disc conversion is the near-universal bulletproofing path.",
      "severity": "high",
      "confidence": "high",
      "symptoms": [
        "Long, soft brake pedal",
        "Brake fade on repeated or downhill stops",
        "Pulling to one side under braking",
        "Grabbing or locking from contaminated/uneven shoes",
        "Difficulty stopping the loaded truck"
      ],
