      "category": "suspension",
      "title": "Worn rear trailing-arm and front control-arm bushings cause float and rear steer",
      "description": "Unlike the 3/4-ton trucks, the half-ton C10 2WD uses a coil-spring rear located by two long (~57 inch) trailing arms plus a panhard bar/track bar. The big rubber trailing-arm bushings (and the front upper/lower control-arm bushings and ball joints) perish with age, letting the rear axle move around under power and braking — a vague, floating ride and a feeling that the rear is steering the truck. Tired front bushings and ball joints add to the wander and eat front tires.",
      "solution": "Replace all the worn bushings — rear trailing-arm bushings front and rear, the panhard/track-bar bushings, and the front upper/lower control-arm bushings — while replacing worn ball joints. Restorers who want sharper handling step up to graphite-impregnated polyurethane (poly) bushings, which are self-lubricating, remove the 'floating' feel and tighten the truck up, and some go to tubular trailing arms/control arms for a full refresh and lowering options. Always do a proper alignment afterward.",
      "severity": "medium",
      "confidence": "high",
      "symptoms": [
        "Floating/wallowing ride and rear-axle 'steer' under power",
        "Clunks from the rear over bumps and on throttle on/off",
        "Front-end wander and clunks, uneven front tire wear",
        "Loose, vague handling on the highway"
      ],
