      "category": "steering",
      "title": "Worn steering box and front-end linkage cause vague, wandering steering",
      "description": "After 50+ years the factory Saginaw recirculating-ball steering box develops slop in the worm/sector, and combined with worn idler arm, tie-rod ends, drag link and ball joints the C10 develops the classic 'wandering' feel where you're constantly correcting and the truck darts on grooved pavement. Many trucks left the factory with manual steering (or the optional ram-assist on early 67s), which only amplifies how heavy and imprecise a tired box feels.",
      "solution": "First replace the worn linkage — idler arm, tie-rod ends, drag link and ball joints — and set the toe, because a fresh box won't fix worn links. Then either rebuild the original box with a Saginaw rebuild kit and re-set the sector lash, or do the popular bulletproof upgrade: swap to a quick-ratio Saginaw 605/500-series power steering box (with the matching pump, pitman arm and frame-dimple confirmed on 68-72). The result is tight, modern on-center feel that transforms how the truck drives.",
      "severity": "medium",
      "confidence": "high",
